05a-803d3465ef0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134386 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=4t\\,i-3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{t}^{3})\\cdot 4t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134864 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134864 #edb/ref 1061 35784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134864 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25733548-0d69-5314-abb7-7cf1ba40a7bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134864 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134865 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134865 #edb/ref 1061 35785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134865 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31e81ee5-877c-5590-999c-e1a635893093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134865 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134866 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134866 #edb/ref 1061 35786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134866 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ff52bb4-279b-55a3-9202-9e4939a84e2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134866 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134867 #edb/ref 1061 35787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134867 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0aca8ded-b498-5e7b-a4b3-06277e60cbf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134867 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134868 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134868 #edb/ref 1061 35792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134868 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6584994-83e5-5672-aa98-e6e1491a031c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134868 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134876 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134876 #edb/ref 1061 35869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134876 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f9c02ea-f6d5-5e40-8f6b-2625f2260d4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134876 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135131 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135131 #edb/ref 1065 5839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135131 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bbaebc78-d8b2-5500-9c52-f82bd3d3a8a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135131 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135132 #edb/ref 1064 "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135132 #edb/ref 1065 5840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135132 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f13baac9-fbd8-5a28-b168-f3010a604b1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135132 #edb/ref 1067 "A Concrete Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135137 #edb/ref 1064 "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =  1,\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135137 #edb/ref 1065 5857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135137 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5c61d2a3-2e54-5e8b-931c-beff07f3581e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135137 #edb/ref 1067 "Negatively Oriented Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428697 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428697 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2198->1181. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428697 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049486 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428698 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428698 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fraction